<h2>The final report and what it actually decides</h2>
<p>Every police investigation ends in a final report to the court under Section 193 of the BNSS (formerly Section 173 CrPC). Where the police believe an offence is made out, the report is a chargesheet; where they do not, it is a closure report — sometimes called a cancellation or untraced report. In a POCSO case the report goes to the Special Court, and Section 33(1) of the POCSO Act gives that court a distinctive power: it may take cognizance of an offence without the accused being committed to it for trial, upon a complaint or upon a police report of the facts. The statute thus places the Special Court at the centre of the decision, with the police report as one input rather than the last word.</p>
<p>The essential point for the family of the child is this: the closure report records the opinion of the investigating officer. The court is not bound by that opinion. It must examine the entire material collected during the investigation — statements, medical records, electronic material, the record of the child's statement — and form its own view.</p>

<h2>The court's three options</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h4>Accept the report</h4><p>If, after hearing the informant, the court agrees that no offence is made out, it accepts the closure report and the proceedings end, subject to the remedies available against that order.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>Disagree and take cognizance</h4><p>The court may disagree with the police conclusion and take cognizance of the offence on the material already collected, notwithstanding the closure opinion. The case then proceeds against the persons the material implicates.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>Direct further investigation</h4><p>Where the investigation is incomplete rather than wrong — witnesses unexamined, forensics not obtained — the court may direct further investigation under Section 193(9) of the BNSS (formerly Section 173(8) CrPC) and require a supplementary report.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Notice to the informant: the hearing that must happen first</h2>
<p>Before a closure report is accepted, the informant must be given notice and an opportunity to be heard. This is settled practice, and it is the procedural hinge on which everything else in this explainer turns: without notice, the family may never learn that closure is proposed until after it has happened. On receiving notice, the informant should promptly obtain copies of the closure report and the accompanying documents, because the protest petition has to engage with what the police actually said, not with what the family assumes they said. In POCSO matters the informant is frequently a parent or guardian acting on behalf of the child, and where the child has a support person appointed under the POCSO framework, that support person can assist the family in understanding the report and the hearing.</p>

<h2>The protest petition</h2>
<p>The protest petition is the informant's formal, reasoned objection to the closure report. It is not a fresh FIR and not a rhetorical document; it is closest in character to a point-by-point rebuttal addressed to a decision-maker who has the full investigation file. A well-drafted protest petition in a POCSO case ordinarily contains:</p>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A para-wise answer to the closure reasoning.</strong> Each ground on which the police propose closure — delay in reporting, supposed inconsistency in the child's account, absence of medical corroboration — is taken up individually and answered from the record.</li>
    <li><strong>The list of uninvestigated leads.</strong> Named witnesses never examined, CCTV or call records never collected, school or neighbourhood enquiries never made, forensic examination never sought.</li>
    <li><strong>Defects in how the child's statement was recorded.</strong> Whether the statutory safeguards for recording a child's statement were followed, and whether the statement was recorded in a setting and manner the law contemplates.</li>
    <li><strong>The specific prayer.</strong> Rejection of the closure report, with cognizance on the existing material; alternatively, further investigation under Section 193(9) BNSS on the identified lapses.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<p>One further possibility deserves mention. Where the material with the protest petition itself discloses the offence, the court may treat the protest petition as a complaint and proceed on it. In the POCSO setting this route fits naturally with Section 33(1), which expressly permits the Special Court to take cognizance upon a complaint. The drafting consequence is that a protest petition should be self-contained: facts in order, documents annexed, and the substance of the allegation complete on its own terms, so that it can stand as a complaint if the court chooses that course.</p>

<h2>Child-friendly considerations throughout</h2>
<p>The protest proceedings involve the same child whose protection the statute exists for, and the remedies should be pursued in a way that does not itself become an ordeal. The guardian or parent acts for the child in the proceedings; a support person, where appointed, provides continuity between the family and the system; and the child need not be produced or re-examined for the purpose of the closure hearing itself, which is argued on the record. Where further investigation is directed, any fresh interaction with the child must follow the child-friendly procedures the statute prescribes for recording statements and medical examination.</p>

<h2>Practical sequence for the family</h2>
<p>Reduced to steps: watch for the notice and never ignore it; obtain the closure report and documents immediately; have the record studied against the original complaint to identify what was not done; file a protest petition that answers the closure reasoning para-wise and specifies the uninvestigated leads; and press, in the alternative, for cognizance on the existing material or further investigation under Section 193(9) BNSS. The closure report is the investigating agency's last word — the statute makes sure it is not the court's first and final one.</p>

<div class="note"><p>Timelines matter. A protest petition should be filed before the court takes up the closure report for orders; if the report has already been accepted without notice or hearing, the remedy shifts to challenging that acceptance, which is a different and more demanding proceeding.</p></div>
